 [["", -0.01083928532898426], ["\n\n", -4.535555839538574]]}], "text": "# CCS Modes on Intel Arc (xe driver)\n\nThe `ccs_mode` parameter controls how Compute Slice (CCS) contexts are handled by the Xe driver. Here's what each mode does:\n\n## CCS Mode 1\n- **Context stealing**: The driver can \"steal\" CCS contexts from other contexts when needed\n- More aggressive resource management\n- Allows better utilization of compute resources by dynamically reallocating CCS\n\n## CCS Mode 2\n- **Static CCS assignment**: CCS contexts are assigned more statically\n- Less dynamic stealing behavior\n- Can provide more predictable behavior in certain workloads\n\n## CCS Mode 4\n- Typically relates to **how CCS contexts are pinned or reserved**\n- Often used for scenarios requiring more stable/dedicated compute resources\n\n---\n\n## Important Caveat\n\nI want to be honest with you: **I'm not fully certain about the exact semantics of these specific mode numbers**, and the `ccs_mode` parameter behavior has evolved across different kernel versions and driver releases. The details above may not be accurate.\n\n## What I'd Recommend\n\nFor accurate, current information, please check:\n\n1. **Kernel documentation**: `Documentation/gpu/xe.rst` or similar in your kernel source\n2. **Driver source**: The `ccs_mode` parameter is defined in the Xe driver source (look for `module_param(ccs_mode)`)\n3. **Intel's documentation**: The Xe driver docs and mailing list discussions\n\nWould you like me to help you locate the actual source or documentation for your specific setup? If you tell me your kernel version, I can give more targeted guidance on where to look."}